3409 is task_get_special_port, and its answer changes

The guest caches MIG replies for a small list of message ids and replays them for later calls
to the same destination. Patch 0068 took 3418 off that list for exactly this reason and the
same argument rules out 3409: a reply may be replayed only for a routine that answers the same
thing every time, and task_set_special_port (3410) writes the very field task_get_special_port
(3409) reads.

launchd calls the two one after the other. runtime_fork sets the job bootstrap port and reads
it back before forking, so the child inherits it. The cache key is (dest, id) and carries
neither the stored port nor which, which lives in the message body, so ONE cached reply answers
every special port for the life of the process.

launchd queries its own bootstrap port at startup, when it correctly has none. That null was
cached and replayed for every later read, and a forked child inherits the cache array, so
_vproc_post_fork_ping sent to MACH_PORT_NULL, got MACH_SEND_INVALID_DEST, and launchd exited
the child with 1 before posix_spawn. No daemon ever ran, which is task #196 and the reason
launchd has been unusable.

Measured before: 103 set messages reached the daemon and only 7 gets, the rest served from the
cache; the kernel held itk_bootstrap non-null while runtime_fork read back 0x0, and 17 children
died with post_fork_ping failed kr=0x10000003. Measured after: readback equals the port that
was set, 26 gets reach the daemon, 0 ping failures, one job exit and it is code 0, and
securityd and memberd are live host processes for the first time.
--- a/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/mach/impl/mach_traps.c
+++ b/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/mach/impl/mach_traps.c
@@ -176,8 +176,24 @@
 	 *
 	 * Measured: the daemon made two semaphores for that process and copied out two distinct names,
 	 * 9731 and 21251, and the caller received 21251 three times and never saw 9731.
+	 *
+	 * 3409 IS task_get_special_port, AND ITS ANSWER CHANGES, which is task #196.
+	 *
+	 * The rule above is the whole story again. task_set_special_port (3410) writes the very value
+	 * 3409 reads, and launchd calls them one after the other: runtime_fork sets the job bootstrap
+	 * port and reads it back before forking, so the child inherits it. The key here is (dest, id)
+	 * and carries neither the port that was stored nor `which`, which lives in the message body,
+	 * so ONE cached reply answers every special port for the life of the process.
+	 *
+	 * launchd queries its own bootstrap port at startup, when it correctly has none. That null was
+	 * cached and replayed for every later read, and forked children inherit the cache array, so
+	 * _vproc_post_fork_ping sent to MACH_PORT_NULL, got MACH_SEND_INVALID_DEST, and launchd
+	 * _exit(1)ed the child before posix_spawn. No daemon ever ran.
+	 *
+	 * Measured: 103 set messages reached the daemon and only 7 gets, because the rest were served
+	 * from this cache; the kernel held itk_bootstrap=0x...450 while the caller kept reading null.
 	 */
-	return id == 200 || id == 206 || id == 3409;
+	return id == 200 || id == 206;
 }
 
